  "items": [
    {
      "id": "adcrush-design-spec",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "ea50dbdb-ef7f-4530-9514-faa64f5c1a42",
          "statement": "Confirmed design: 5 independent vectors (narrative_format, canon, style, duration, platform); gate cycle (init/intake/check/canon-gate/breakdown/expand_technical/compile/render guide+final/audit/publish); LLM touchpoints limited to check, breakdown, expand_technical, review; assemble_prompts and verify_structure stay pure deterministic; per-block hash-lock with approved-as-content-hash-property invalidation (global style change invalidates everything, local element change invalidates only dependents); MCP exposes 19 CLI commands 1:1, no separate chat tool; generalized viewer is read+annotate+export only (never mutates state, decisions happen in chat), served locally on a fixed greedy-reserved port, localStorage scoped by project+revision+payload slug; publish is purely local (no git/deploy dependency) to adcrush/exports/<name>-<id>/<timestamp>/output/; kie-api-python installed via uv, invoked as an accessory CLI subprocess, never imported. Concrete schemas defined for narrative_format, canon, style_profile (proven with a chibi-comic worked example reusing verbatim 29-razones text), and block."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "adcrush-milestone-plan",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "cbaa6c93-495c-42f8-8c0b-559c0c4a386a",
          "statement": "Reconciled M0-M10 milestone chain for implementing adcrush: M0 uv-managed scaffold + .mcp-adcrush/ workspace (git-style parent walk-up, slugified default name, refuses nesting under an existing ancestor); M1 intake with versioned attachments; M2 provider-agnostic multimodal LLM adapter with retry discipline and an inferred/missing split in check's output; M3 kie-cli as an accessory subprocess reading credentials from adcrush's own .env; M4 canon gate as shared library code (never a blocking synchronous call) plus a dedicated sheet-prompt template; M5 breakdown/expand/compile with three named invalidation scopes (global/local/narrative); M6 render guide/final queue; M7 forked/adapted viewer served locally, browser-launch left to the calling agent; M8 audit/publish with a revision-mixing correctness check; M9 official Python mcp SDK over stdio; M10 style gallery with capped, LLM-assisted iteration. A uniform JSON success/error envelope applies across all 19 commands."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "audio-four-layers-three-generators",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "a673bd7d-683a-4e50-9756-966d9ce21d69",
          "statement": "Audio is FOUR independently configured layers but only THREE generators. Speech (audio.speech: auto|none|video_model|tts|both) comes from the video model for lipsync or ElevenLabs for narration. Sound effects (audio.sfx, default ON) come from the VIDEO ENGINE ALWAYS -- user decision, because diegetic sound must land on the action and only the model that generated the action knows when the foil crinkles or the door shuts. suno-sounds exists in kie-cli and is deliberately NOT used, because separately generated sfx would have to be placed against the EDL by hand, trading a real defect (drift) for a cosmetic one. Music (audio.music, default off) comes from suno_music. audio.muted forces all three off. Since Seedance's --generate-audio is one toggle for voice and sfx together, the common voice-over case keeps audio ON to get sfx, instructs the prompt to speak no dialogue, polices the bed with a new UNWANTED_SPEECH code, and ducks it under the narration. Disentanglement is therefore at the level of configuration and mixing, never generation."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "audio-intent-modes",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "959aa213-0dc1-4576-b2d9-340c2d6cdeb5",
          "statement": "A project declares VideoAudioConfig.audio_mode as one of voice_over (muted characters, narration over picture, speech from ElevenLabs), live_lipsync (characters speak on camera, speech from the video model), or voice_over_and_live_lipsync (both, mixed separately). DialogueLine.mode gains 'narration'. Classified once by the LLM at breakdown/expand from the brief and beats, persisted, and user-overridable, never silently re-derived. It must land before further video spend because it changes what the video model is asked for. Open constraint: Seedance's --generate-audio is one toggle for voice AND sfx, so voice_over mode cannot ask for sfx without speech; recommendation is to keep audio on, instruct no spoken dialogue, and add an UNWANTED_SPEECH defect code so the judge polices it."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "audio-layers-implemented",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "d5fc8096-8344-40fa-b7c0-8e045672cdd8",
          "statement": "audio/layers.py owns resolve() as a pure function and prompt_clause() as its only text output; video.py delegates both, so nothing re-derives the mapping. VideoAudioConfig is now speech (auto|none|video_model|tts|both) / sfx (default on, always from the video engine) / background_music / audio_mode / muted, and DialogueLine.mode gained 'narration' for copy spoken by a voice that is not on screen. audio_mode is classified once at breakdown from the whole brief, because a product-only ad with no people must be voice_over and never live_lipsync; it is returned as a PROPOSAL and accepted only when the project has neither an explicit speech source nor an existing audio_mode, so re-running breakdown never undoes a human decision."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "canon-describe",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "65ab88dd-ed3a-43ce-a5b4-6f409d448e74",
          "statement": "adcrush canon describe --element --description corrects an element's description, records a canon.described ledger event, and DEMOTES the element to candidate with content_hash cleared. The demotion is the point: approved means a human accepted the SHEET, and the sheet still shows the old text; the hash is recomputed by generate, the step that knows the style and reference images it covers. Every block referencing the element therefore reads as stale, which is the honest consequence of changing what the element is. Replaces hand-editing canon.json, which bypasses the ledger and reads as an unexplained manual override."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "canon-grammar-is-medium-neutral",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "aa8c35e7-5bc3-4721-b1d5-4c351904b4a5",
          "statement": "CANON_FIDELITY_INTRO no longer opens with 'photoreal' and CANON_FIDELITY_FOOTER no longer says 'do not restyle'. Both were live-action assumptions stated as universal rules, and both contradicted the campaign treatment that the same canon grammar injects two sections later; because the intro came first it won. A motion-graphics campaign therefore got photoreal product photography for its canon, and every final conditioned on those sheets came out photoreal too. Fidelity in the canon grammar now means identity, proportion, material and colour, rendered in whatever medium the campaign's treatment states. Canon sheet ART is deliberately excluded from content_hash, so this fix does not invalidate existing sheets: a regeneration must be forced deliberately."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "canon-sheets-have-a-scope",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "181ef565-7066-4d58-81a9-800cdc52c916",
          "statement": "compile.canon_precedence_clause(style) states what the attached canon sheets are authority FOR: identity, proportion, colour and printed lettering, and NOT how they are rendered, because a sheet is drawn plainly so those facts stay readable and therefore looks photographic. Attached with nothing said, the model treats the whole sheet as the target and a stylised campaign renders photoreal while following every word of its treatment -- observed as STYLE_BREAK 0.62 on a block whose state lock already said 'no specular glints'. Returns empty for a style that already is photoreal, where the sentence would only cost prompt length. The video prompt's REFERENCE MAP header carries the same scoping."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "caption-char-budget",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "2ebc9955-2941-4468-81ad-2484f022cfc4",
          "statement": "ass.char_budget(style, canvas) computes how many characters physically fit one line, from the canvas width less the 8 percent horizontal margins and size_pct times frame height times CHAR_WIDTH_RATIO 0.62, and the smaller of that and the style's max_chars_per_line wins. The two are different numbers: max_chars_per_line is an editorial preference and was being used as the physical limit, so the motion-graphics register's 20 characters at size_pct 6.0 (about 1000px of Arial Black against 604px of usable width) shipped clipped at both frame edges. WrapStyle 2 means libass does not wrap, so anything too wide is silently CLIPPED rather than reflowed. The ratio is measured against Arial Black uppercase, the widest face any register asks for, so every other face breaks slightly early -- the safe direction. Budgets on 720x1280: motion-graphics 12, ugc-vertical 15, papercraft 16. Resolution-independent by construction since size_pct is a fraction of frame height; a 16:9 frame fits the style's full preference. The karaoke path had the same bug and never wrapped at all; it now breaks on the visible length, ignoring the \\\\k override tags. The guard test asserts for every style and register that budget times size_px times ratio fits the usable width, rather than freezing a golden file."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "captions-are-ass-post-production",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "959aa213-0dc1-4576-b2d9-340c2d6cdeb5",
          "statement": "Captions are generated deterministically as ASS and burned with ffmpeg's 'ass' filter, never the 'subtitles' filter, which renders text but discards ASS animation and positioning. Verified on this machine: ffmpeg 7.1.1 built with libass, libfreetype, libfontconfig and libharfbuzz, both filters present, 2819 system fonts, so there is NO new runtime dependency. CaptionStyle becomes a struct (font_family, size_pct, fill, outline_colour, outline_px, shadow_px, position, animation none|word_pop|karaoke_wipe|bounce, max_chars_per_line, max_lines) and every major style must declare THREE substyles -- default, loud, quiet -- documented in a new docs/style-manual.md as a requirement of a style definition. An LLM computes the caption EDL (phrase grouping and per-word offsets) from the caption text plus shot timecodes; Stage 5 replaces the estimated word timings with ElevenLabs' real ones. StyleProfile.on_screen_text changes meaning from 'ask the model for text' to 'burn captions in post', generators are told there is no text at all, and TEXT_BURNED_IN inverts to fire when a generator produced text it was told not to."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "captions-are-post-production",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "6f17b960-53a5-4b10-8d38-f4dd650ddb8f",
          "statement": "Captions are planned by captions/edl.py, rendered by captions/ass.py and burned by captions/burn.py with ffmpeg's 'ass' filter as the last step of video-assemble, after the join trim. Composition onto the assembled timeline uses KEPT lengths, not clip lengths: trimming a join shortens what precedes it, so offsetting by clip length puts every caption after the first join progressively late. Captions never enter a held handle, because a caption there would be trimmed away. Word timings are labelled 'estimated' and are proportional to word length; the narration stage replaces them with measured ones. An LLM is consulted only for phrase grouping of a line too long for the style's budget, and its answer is REJECTED unless every word survives in order -- a caption is copy, and a model paraphrasing it is a defect."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "captions-never-reach-a-generator",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "e770ffe1-33e9-40b9-8eee-0c6056b0ab5f",
          "statement": "Two layers. (1) The expand system prompt carries an explicit CAPTIONS ARE POST-PRODUCTION clause: copy goes in dialogue and nowhere else, because a caption is not something the camera sees, while words printed on the physical product are. (2) compile.strip_on_screen_text_directives removes whole sentences carrying an on-screen-text cue (caption, on-screen text, title card, text overlay, supertitle, subtitle) from beat, action, state_lock, entry_state/exit_state, shot_line and the video EDL relevo note. Sentence level because a generator handed half a sentence invents the rest; applied at prompt-assembly time so blocks.json keeps the author's words and no block hash moves. The cue list excludes bare 'reads' and 'lettering' because product packaging legitimately carries print. The invariant asserted in simulation is that no prompt carries a caption DIRECTIVE, never that the copy is absent: a speech video_model group is legitimately told to speak the line, and short copy like 'Made in USA.' is often the pack's own print."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "draft-final-model-tiering",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "2845317c-d9ef-4b54-b1d3-70eb5e8c3b31",
          "statement": "video-loop takes --model/--draft-model (default seedance-2-fast) and --final-model (default seedance-2.5). The judge/retry ladder runs on the draft; every group whose review_status is 'pass' is re-rendered ONCE on the final model by media_review/tiering.py. Rejected and exhausted cuts are never re-rendered, because paying the premium for a cut the judge already rejected buys nothing. The final render is judged too, since a different model is a different model rather than a strictly better one, and on failure the draft stays promoted -- there is no ladder at this tier so failing safe means keeping what passed. The draft is preserved as <group>.draft.mp4, the only evidence of what the cheap model produced for the same prompt. Draft and final lanes are the same lane class with different model values, so transport, reference plan and rubric are shared by construction. Deliberate limitation: the reference plan is computed once from the DRAFT model's 9 slots, so the final render reuses those rather than 2.5's 30 -- same prompt, same references, different model, because changing inputs between tiers would make the comparison unattributable. Set --final-model equal to --model to disable."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "elevenlabs-tts-facts",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "959aa213-0dc1-4576-b2d9-340c2d6cdeb5",
          "statement": "elevenlabs/text-to-speech-multilingual-v2 runs on kie's normal market endpoints. Input: text (<=5000), voice (id or preset name), stability 0-1 (0.5), similarity_boost 0-1 (0.75), style 0-1 (0), speed 0.7-1.2 (1), timestamps bool (false), previous_text/next_text (<=5000), language_code (ISO 639-1). 68 documented voices with ids, names and character descriptions; default EkK5I93UQWFDigLMpZcX (James). Previews are plain files at https://static.aiquickdraw.com/elevenlabs/voice/<voice_id>.mp3, so a preview UI needs no backend. Two parameters are structurally important: timestamps=true returns WORD-LEVEL timings, which become the caption sync source so no transcription is needed anywhere; and previous_text/next_text keep prosody continuous across per-block narration segments, which is how the copy is split to match shot durations. kie-cli has NO ElevenLabs support today (routes.py knows only market, veo, suno), so Stage 5 needs an upstream PR of the same three-blocker shape as GPT Image 2.5."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "elevenlabs-upstream-and-narration",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "fdfdcaab-3749-4432-a783-de4752cd9354",
          "statement": "kie-api-python PR #2 (squash-merged to main as 878c88b) adds elevenlabs/text-to-speech-multilingual-v2 and elevenlabs/text-to-dialogue-v3, a speech subcommand, a voices helper and MCP parity -- the same three blockers as GPT Image 2.5, including the MARKET_MODELS routing one that would raise on wait after a BILLED submit. adcrush side: kie.submit_speech defaults timestamps ON, because a segment rendered without them cannot be used for caption sync and paying twice is the only alternative; audio/narration.py owns the SpeechProvider protocol, gives every segment its neighbours via previous_text/next_text so prosody does not restart each shot, and runs a bounded fit loop -- two speed adjustments inside the provider's 0.7-1.2 range, then it REPORTS the overrun rather than truncating, because cutting audio halves a word and choosing between tightening copy and lengthening a shot is editorial. Timing parsing tolerates several plausible response shapes and returns no words rather than crashing on an unknown one."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "elevenlabs-voice-field-takes-the-id",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "c04f61e7-cc43-4b8f-a400-3cdb3dd1905e",
          "statement": "kie's ElevenLabs voice field takes the voice ID. v3 validates it at submit and returns 422 naming the bad value; v2 accepts any string and fails minutes later with an opaque 500. CORRECTION to an earlier speculation in this stream that the field might want the voice NAME: that was read off one name-based submission getting further than an id-based one, which was noise -- both fail identically. The pipeline was already sending ids and never had this defect. Practical consequence for diagnosis: a v3 submit is a free, instant, specific check of a voice id that v2 will silently swallow."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "expand-force",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "7dde0fd8-80a2-44bf-9aa6-0adcb4bb2c51",
          "statement": "expand --all targets only never-expanded or canon-stale blocks, so it cannot see a block that went stale because the expand SCHEMA gained a field -- blocks expanded before hook_device existed look current. expand --force re-derives every block, deliberately explicit because it changes every block hash and forces a full re-render."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "expand-is-written-for-the-medium",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "e770ffe1-33e9-40b9-8eee-0c6056b0ab5f",
          "statement": "run_expand_technical takes style= and prepends compile.medium_guidance(style), which quotes the treatment and asks for terms the medium actually has: no depth of field, bokeh or flare without a physical lens; no specular highlights or subsurface sheen in a flat graphic medium; cut edges and paper grain rather than skin pores in papercraft; say what MOVES and what CHANGES, which every medium can express. Positive rather than prohibitive because a banned-word list is endless and brittle -- bokeh is only wrong in a medium without a lens. It closes by naming the arithmetic: the treatment is two lines at the top of the render prompt and the expansion is four paragraphs below it, so whichever medium those words describe is the one that gets rendered. Optional, so a caller predating it keeps working."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "face-policy-is-settable",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "c8575d29-c8df-44f8-94c9-8a844faaab01",
          "statement": "canon generate takes --face-policy {infer,unrestricted,never} (CLI, MCP and generate_candidate). Excluded from content_hash like physical_profile, so setting it patches an approved element for free. It also DECIDES the character archetype: _is_partial_character reads face_policy first and only falls back to the description keywords for 'infer'. The keyword guess had misfired visibly -- an on-camera creator whose description mentions 'adult hands, short clean nails' was handed the hands-only character-partial archetype and rendered a sheet with no turntable. Before this flag existed, the only way to put a character on camera was to hand-edit canon.json."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "held-handles-and-honest-duration",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "e733aad4-8e12-48cc-b940-1106ad8499a9",
          "statement": "EdlEntry gains hold_head and hold_tail, set by allocate_timecodes(handle_seconds=, hold_head=, hold_tail=) ONLY at clip ends that actually abut another group; a single-group ad reserves nothing. The timecodes are untouched because the clip is still generated at full length, and the hold reaches the model as a shot clause. video-assemble takes --trim-seconds (default 0.5) and trims internal joins only via the pure trim_plan(count, trim); _trim_clip re-encodes rather than stream-copies because -ss on a copy snaps to the nearest keyframe, and it takes the kept length from the group's PLANNED duration rather than probing, so a clip returning 15.07s has its overrun trimmed instead of making every join a different length. CORRECTION to an earlier claim in the plan: handles do NOT let a 30s brief deliver 30s. Each join removes 2 x trim and the generation length is capped by the model, so a two-group 30s ad delivers 29.0s. video-assemble now reports planned_duration_seconds and duration_seconds separately, and --trim-seconds 0 gives an exact-length concat."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "hooks-are-a-contract",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "09cccc0b-1a67-4658-9881-7b4ef2fb7785",
          "statement": "Block.hook_device is written by expand for the OPENING block only and is hashed, so a changed hook re-renders. It is in EXPAND_SCHEMA but deliberately NOT in its required list -- demanding it everywhere would teach the model to invent one for shot 4 -- and run_expand_technical appends HOOK_GUIDANCE and enforces presence only when the block opens the ad and the format asks for a hook, failing hook_device_missing otherwise. A non-opening block has hook_device CLEARED even if the model volunteers one, because left in place it would bias a mid-ad shot toward an opening it is not. compile.calling_image_clause is keyed on hook_device rather than on order == 1, so hook_required: false costs nothing and the clause can never contradict an empty hook. HOOK_WEAK (floor major) has a full recipe ladder, and the judge rubric line is carried only by the block that has a hook, because a calm mid-ad shot judged against a hook rubric would flag the whole ad. Correction to an earlier claim: hook_required was not 'never read' -- it reached ONE sentence of the breakdown prompt; nothing downstream acted on it, which is the part that mattered."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "image-model-gpt-image-2-5",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "39b4e6d5-8729-4719-abd9-75d2049ede11",
          "statement": "adcrush's default image model is kie.DEFAULT_IMAGE_MODEL = 'gpt-image-2-5-sunburst' (GPT Image 2.5, Sunburst tier), replacing eight hardcoded 'gpt-image-2' literals. kie-cli resolves the alias to -text-to-image or -image-to-image from whether images are attached, so adcrush's f'{model}-{suffix}' status-model derivation is unchanged. Flare ('gpt-image-2-5-flare') stays available per call for drafts. submit_image gained an optional background passthrough (transparent|opaque|auto), omitted unless set. Upstream support is kie-api-python PR #1, squash-merged to main as 5c9fb10; adcrush keeps installing from main."
