      "id": "video-self-improvement-s10-keyframes",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "2696efbc-a98e-46ec-a8b3-c7cb977b65c3",
          "statement": "S10 keyframe expansion: per planned shot, FIRST/MID/LAST 2K keyframes at the campaign aspect derived by the LLM from F# + the EDL line; first/last carry the first/last caption line when the style burns captions in, MID never; slugs K:{order}-first|mid|last replace F# in the reference plan when complete; attached as first/last frames + references only for models flagged supports_first_last_with_references (none today), else all references; runbook video-plan -> render-assembly -> keyframes -> video-plan -> video-loop."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "voice-catalog-and-selection",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "fdfdcaab-3749-4432-a783-de4752cd9354",
          "statement": "audio/voices.yaml holds 67 voices with the vendor's id, name and character description plus OUR tags and good_for, which is the piece the vendor docs lack. The catalog is 67 entries, not the 68 the kie docs claim: the page lists 67 rows, so one is a duplicate or a doc slip, recorded rather than padded because a fabricated entry is worse than a known gap. STYLE_AFFINITY maps each style to the tags that suit it, as data, so a new style is one row. propose() returns a ranked shortlist WITH the reason each entry scored, never one silent answer, because voice is a taste decision and the job is to narrow 67 options and explain why. A chosen voice persists as audio.voice_id -- named voice_id and not voice, because that name belonged to the legacy boolean the migration validator pops and reusing it would make one key mean two types depending on the file's age. An unknown configured voice FAILS rather than falling back: shipping an ad in a stranger's voice is worse than stopping. adcrush voice list|preview|set, with a self-contained audition page needing no API call and no backend."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "voice-over-safe-copy",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "19876af6-48ff-46db-9217-1d7eab4ade46",
          "statement": "DialogueLine gains voice_over, the SPOKEN form of text, and audio/speech_safety.py normalises the common cases while refusing the rest. No new dependency: a number-to-words covering ad copy plus a units table beats pulling in a locale model. Money is read as units and subunits, so $9.45 is 'nine dollars forty-five' and never 'nine point four five dollars', which is what a generic decimal rule produces and what no narrator would say. Ambiguity is LISTED rather than guessed: AMBIGUOUS_ABBREVS holds short forms with more than one reading (m metre or minute, t tonne or teaspoon, c cup or Celsius, tsp, min) and each demands an explicit voice_over, because putting them in UNITS would be choosing for the user. An explicit voice_over always wins over normalisation. The gate is _require_speakable_copy in run_video_loop -- the last free moment before spend, not plan time which is free and should never block -- and it applies whoever speaks, since the video model reads the copy too when it provides the dialogue."
