          "statement": "assemble_final_video wraps the narration attempt: on AdcrushError it finishes the cut without narration, burns the captions on the planner's estimated timings, and records narration.failed with the vendor's code in both the ledger event and the caller's result. Re-running video-assemble once the provider recovers adds the voice and regenerates no clips. The posture generalises: the expensive, irreversible artifacts are the generated clips, so a cheap optional layer failing must never invalidate them."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "narration-in-delivery",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "7dde0fd8-80a2-44bf-9aa6-0adcb4bb2c51",
          "statement": "video.assemble_final_video takes audio= and speech_provider=. When audio.layers.resolve(...).speech is tts or both it renders one narration segment per block via audio/providers.py, places each at an explicit adelay through audio/mix.place_segments, ducks the clip's own bed to BED_GAIN 0.35 with mix_narration (never replaces it -- the provider's audio flag stays on for sfx), and records runs/<rev>/video/narration/narration.json. The DELAY for each block is the same number that block's first caption starts on (captions.edl.block_offsets), so audio and captions cannot drift: one derivation, used twice. Ordering is plan captions -> render narration -> adopt measured timings -> burn; burning first would freeze the planner's estimate into the deliverable while the real read was already known. No provider is constructed unless the run is --live --yes, so a dry run still produces the cut and reports the skip in the ledger."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "narrator-reads-the-on-screen-copy",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "7dde0fd8-80a2-44bf-9aa6-0adcb4bb2c51",
          "statement": "captions.edl.caption_lines is the single predicate for 'this line is on screen' (modes cartela and narration), and video.narration_segments projects from it rather than applying its own rule. In a voice-over ad the on-screen copy IS the script, and every project written before the narration mode existed keeps its copy in cartela -- selecting only narration rendered nothing on the canonical scenario. dialogo is excluded: the video model performs a character speaking in shot. A line whose printed form does not normalise and that carries no voice_over is skipped rather than guessed at; speech_safety.check is what surfaces those."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "no-generator-is-asked-for-text",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "6f17b960-53a5-4b10-8d38-f4dd650ddb8f",
          "statement": "Every generator is now told there is no text in frame beyond what is physically printed on the product: the video prompt unconditionally, compile._dialogue_section (captions and narration removed, only performed speech survives because it changes the pose), _caption_mode_section (returns nothing), and keyframes.caption_for (always None, because a lettered keyframe becomes a reference teaching the video model to letter too). Copy reaches a video prompt only as a spoken cue, and only when the video model is the one speaking -- otherwise the clip would speak over the narration laid on top. On the judge side the 'required caption present and correctly spelled' question is gone, which is what produced five false TEXT_GARBLED flags on one live pass, two over the confidence gate and each costing a render, because a caption fading in over the first second looks missing in a sampled frame. TEXT_BURNED_IN now means ANY text not printed on the product; TEXT_GARBLED narrows to product lettering, which still matters because a misspelled pack is a brand defect."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "one-no-text-rule-for-both-lanes",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "181ef565-7066-4d58-81a9-800cdc52c916",
          "statement": "video.NO_TEXT_RULE is a single string used by the video prompt and by every final image prompt (compile.no_text_rule()), widened to name headlines, stat callouts and buttons alongside captions, subtitles, titles and watermarks. The image lane had never had it: a 'final lockup' beat came back as a finished static ad with a REAL COFFEE. MORE YOU. headline, stat callouts and a SHOP NOW button, none of it requested, at TEXT_BURNED_IN critical 0.97. A model that knows what ads look like volunteers the typography unless told the frame is not where it goes."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "papercraft-style",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "c8575d29-c8df-44f8-94c9-8a844faaab01",
          "statement": "New style papercraft.yaml, generalized from the client reference reel YouChews_Papercraft_Unbuild.mp4 (1080x1920, 24s). Describes the craft and the tabletop only, never a product or ingredient: every object sculpted from matte paper on a seamless warm-paper ground, straight-down or high three-quarter framing, soft even top light with gentle contact shadows, warm neutral palette, camera_rig tripod, its own caption_style, on_screen_text true. Any campaign can adopt it."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "print-critical-references",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "0ffda16f-bd54-414f-aff8-950ee3b90efb",
          "statement": "CanonElement.print_critical declares that an element carries printed copy which must be reproduced exactly, so its SHEET is the only source of that wording. build_reference_plan ranks print-critical sheets ahead of every other canon sheet, so reference truncation cannot reach them; canon generate --print-critical sets it. Declared rather than inferred from the description, for the same reason face_policy is: reading prose for the word 'copy' fails silently and in English only. Excluded from compute_element_hash -- a fact about how the element must be REFERENCED, not about what the art is -- so declaring it never forces a billed re-render. The underlying principle: a shape survives being described ('a low flat square slab, width clearly greater than thickness' is actionable prose and the shape-lock bullets work), but thirteen lines of pack copy reach a generator as thirteen chances to invent. Reference priority is therefore not one fixed order; it depends on whether the fact a sheet carries can travel in words at all."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "product-truth-beats-invention",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "c8575d29-c8df-44f8-94c9-8a844faaab01",
          "statement": "At the canon gate the expand LLM's product descriptions were overridden with the approved product truth: it had proposed a 3cm rounded chew with a small wordmark where the real chew is a flat 2.5cm square under all-over tiled foil. The papercraft reference reel renders the chew as a ROUND rosette; the canon keeps the real flat square and carries the rosette as a die-cut pattern on its top face. The real 30-count pouch is DARK COFFEE-BROWN with a gold front panel, NOT black: both new projects' pouch canon was regenerated with the full front copy transcribed from the photo. This matters beyond the sheet, because the description feeds the judge's rubric -- 'black pouch' would make the judge flag a correct render as CANON_DRIFT and burn video retries. coffee-chews-ugc-v2's youchews_retail_bag_sealed still says black and should be corrected."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "prompt-language-positive",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "39b4e6d5-8729-4719-abd9-75d2049ede11",
          "statement": "Generator-facing prompt text is written as description of the wanted shot, not as stacked prohibitions: FRAMING_RULE, IMPLIED_FACE_ACTION_RULE, SHAPE_LOCK_SHARED_BULLET, the caption/anti-bleed/hard-cut constraints and the clip-endpoint line. PhysicalProfile.must_not is no longer rendered into any generator prompt; it reaches the judge's rubric verbatim instead. The reason is mechanical: a diffusion model told 'no cube' draws a cube, while the judge is a language model and reads prohibitions correctly. The HARD CONSTRAINTS header is unchanged because recipes.split_shots keys on it."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "resumable-stages-need-a-driver",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "aa8c35e7-5bc3-4721-b1d5-4c351904b4a5",
          "statement": "render-assembly and keyframes take --no-wait, --poll-interval and --max-wait, and wait by default. Each underlying cycle is ONE bounded pass that submits, reports what is in flight as pending, and returns; the --review stages are driven to completion by media_review.loop.run_until_settled, but these two had no driver. A single call therefore left everything pending, and a runbook that stepped straight on to video-plan planned WITHOUT the G# and K# references those stages exist to produce. cli._drive_to_completion repeats the pass until nothing is pending, it halts, or max_wait expires; each pass is idempotent and never resubmits an in-flight job."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "shape-lock-bullets-rank-print-critical",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "c603706e-d53d-4e61-a620-4c9b947a5f01",
          "statement": "_shape_lock_bullets states print-critical elements first and MAX_SHAPE_LOCK_BULLETS is 5 (was 3). Taking the first N in reference order let three chew-and-wrapper elements fill the cap while the retail pouch -- the hero prop of the failing shot -- had no constraint line anywhere in the prompt. The cap is a length guard, not a ranking; 5 bullets cost about 200 characters against 2.3k of headroom under the 6k budget. Same principle as the reference plan one layer up. The fallback chain already reaches physical_profile.must, so an element carrying only a positive must does get a line -- the ingredient set's counts had simply been cut by the same cap, which is why it came back with five beans and an invented halved coconut."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "stylised-groups-keep-a-style-exemplar",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "65ab88dd-ed3a-43ce-a5b4-6f409d448e74",
          "statement": "build_reference_plan takes style= and promotes the FIRST shot's F# to just after the storyboards when compile.is_stylised_medium(style). Without it a nine-slot model filled up with G + three B sheets + five canon sheets and dropped every final, leaving the video model with sketches and plainly-lit turntables and no example of the medium -- the same failure the image lane had one stage upstream. The original 'finals last' ordering was reasoned on a photoreal campaign, where the canon sheets already are the look, and that case keeps it. is_stylised_medium is shared with canon_precedence_clause so the two cannot disagree about what stylised means."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "two-caption-adoption-levels",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "7dde0fd8-80a2-44bf-9aa6-0adcb4bb2c51",
          "statement": "captions.edl.adopt_measured_timings returns the track plus a per-block mode. A block whose measured word count equals its captions' word count is retimed word for word (measured). A block whose counts differ -- the NORMAL case for number-heavy copy, since 100mg is one caption word and three spoken ones -- has its captions linearly warped onto the measured duration from the block's own offset (fitted): phrase bounds become correct, the intra-phrase sweep stays an estimate. Dropping back to the estimate in that case, as the first implementation did, would have left exactly the copy stage 4 exists for unsynced. Track-level timings is measured when every block matched, fitted when some were warped, mixed when some block had no narration at all."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "video-self-improvement-captions",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "2696efbc-a98e-46ec-a8b3-c7cb977b65c3",
          "statement": "UGC styles (ugc, ugc-vertical) burn editor captions into finals, keyframes and video: exact text in the caption style (bold white sans, drop shadow, upper-middle safe zone); the judge requires them (missing/illegible -> TEXT_GARBLED) and flags only other text as TEXT_BURNED_IN; styles with on_screen_text=false keep the previous VO-only behavior."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "video-self-improvement-lane",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "8a13cafe-b985-40f2-9eb0-6ef90a61700e",
          "statement": "Contract for the video self-improvement lane: every generated sheet carries a unified printed slug (six-zone layout catalog, guide vs canon grammar, campaign-format canvas); video prompts become referential 15s EDLs (<=3.5k chars, hard 6k) addressing sheets by slug/@Image index; total duration must be a multiple of 15s and groups exactly 15s; a media_review engine judges clips/images with Anthropic vision and retries at most 3 times with additive recipes (intensity 1 soft, 2 hard, 3 may simplify the beat); an append-only JSON ledger feeds a new adcrush dashboard; adcrush stays the CLI boundary; approved canon sheets are never regenerated; no assemble/music in this lane."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "video-self-improvement-s10-keyframes",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "2696efbc-a98e-46ec-a8b3-c7cb977b65c3",
          "statement": "S10 keyframe expansion: per planned shot, FIRST/MID/LAST 2K keyframes at the campaign aspect derived by the LLM from F# + the EDL line; first/last carry the first/last caption line when the style burns captions in, MID never; slugs K:{order}-first|mid|last replace F# in the reference plan when complete; attached as first/last frames + references only for models flagged supports_first_last_with_references (none today), else all references; runbook video-plan -> render-assembly -> keyframes -> video-plan -> video-loop."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "voice-catalog-and-selection",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "fdfdcaab-3749-4432-a783-de4752cd9354",
          "statement": "audio/voices.yaml holds 67 voices with the vendor's id, name and character description plus OUR tags and good_for, which is the piece the vendor docs lack. The catalog is 67 entries, not the 68 the kie docs claim: the page lists 67 rows, so one is a duplicate or a doc slip, recorded rather than padded because a fabricated entry is worse than a known gap. STYLE_AFFINITY maps each style to the tags that suit it, as data, so a new style is one row. propose() returns a ranked shortlist WITH the reason each entry scored, never one silent answer, because voice is a taste decision and the job is to narrow 67 options and explain why. A chosen voice persists as audio.voice_id -- named voice_id and not voice, because that name belonged to the legacy boolean the migration validator pops and reusing it would make one key mean two types depending on the file's age. An unknown configured voice FAILS rather than falling back: shipping an ad in a stranger's voice is worse than stopping. adcrush voice list|preview|set, with a self-contained audition page needing no API call and no backend."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "id": "voice-over-safe-copy",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "record": "19876af6-48ff-46db-9217-1d7eab4ade46",
          "statement": "DialogueLine gains voice_over, the SPOKEN form of text, and audio/speech_safety.py normalises the common cases while refusing the rest. No new dependency: a number-to-words covering ad copy plus a units table beats pulling in a locale model. Money is read as units and subunits, so $9.45 is 'nine dollars forty-five' and never 'nine point four five dollars', which is what a generic decimal rule produces and what no narrator would say. Ambiguity is LISTED rather than guessed: AMBIGUOUS_ABBREVS holds short forms with more than one reading (m metre or minute, t tonne or teaspoon, c cup or Celsius, tsp, min) and each demands an explicit voice_over, because putting them in UNITS would be choosing for the user. An explicit voice_over always wins over normalisation. The gate is _require_speakable_copy in run_video_loop -- the last free moment before spend, not plan time which is free and should never block -- and it applies whoever speaks, since the video model reads the copy too when it provides the dialogue."
        }
      ],
      "unresolved": false
    }
  ],
